# Every claim that spans a width, beside whether it claims to have none. One row per ranged claim
# anywhere in a document, `violates` where narrowsWhen is `notApplicable`.
#
# The mirror of checks/narrows_a_point_value.sqlc
#   The pair is why both are worth having. `notApplicable` on a narrowing means "there is no
#   range here to narrow"; filed against a claim that demonstrably spans one, it is a category
#   error the document can see for itself.
#
# Every claim, not only demands
#   Confined to `layer.demand_*`, this rule cannot reach the case the mirror rule's comment
#   predicts, and `every-local-part` filed one: an elimination of 1800/2000/2400 answering
#   `notApplicable`, under a note pasted verbatim from the elimination beside it, which is
#   genuinely a point value (2160 exactly) where the same note is true. One document, two claims,
#   and only the width tells them apart. Widened to every claim, the rule reaches it, and the
#   honest answer there is `unmeasured`, which is what that filing's own source demand says.
#
# The passing idiom for a ranged elimination
#   It is in the corpus twice and worth copying: `instrument` with the measurement that would
#   tighten it, over a `none` bound origin: "nothing sets this bound. It is the size of the
#   double counting the composer found".
-- pm:Claim/pm:narrowsWhen with pm:absent/reason = notApplicable on any claim where low <> high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.layer,
           c.narrows_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'notApplicable' AS violates,
           format('%s claim %s: spans %s to %s %s, yet says there is no range to narrow',
                  c.owns, c.seq, c.low, c.high, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/ranged_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'range_says_no_range'
